= What Nation-Building Meant in 1947

Independence arrived as a package deal with two enormous tasks. First, India was not yet a single political unit — British provinces and 565 princely states had to be melted into one country. Second, that one country had to be held together WITHOUT force — through democracy, the very system Europe thought a poor, divided society could not sustain. Nehru's generation had to do both at once.

- *The two challenges the NCERT names:*
  - *Shape a united nation:* the map was a mosaic — provinces ruled directly by the British, princely states ruled by kings, and communities split by the new Pakistan border.
  - *Establish democracy:* the Constituent Assembly was writing the Constitution while the country burned — and elections on universal franchise were promised within four years.

- *Nehru's method — accommodation, not uniformity:*
  - India's identity would be *unity in diversity*: many languages, faiths and cultures inside one constitutional frame — not one language or one religion imposed on all.
  - *Secularism as a practical promise:* after Partition's communal bloodbath, Muslims who stayed in India had to feel (and be treated as) equal citizens — not as a suspect minority.
  - *Institutions over charisma:* the Election Commission, the Constitution, federal bargaining — democracy was to rest on rules that outlive any leader.
  - *Where force was used, it was surgical:* Hyderabad's police action, for instance — the default tool was negotiation.

#analogy-box(title: "The Renovation Job")[
  Nation-building in 1947 was like renovating a huge ancestral house DURING a monsoon: the roof (unity) leaked, the wiring (administration) was colonial-era, and half the family (Pakistan) had just moved out taking furniture (Partition). The mapping: the mosaic map = the cracked walls; refugees = the displaced relatives needing rooms; language quarrels = disputes over which nameplate hangs on the door. You cannot stop the rain — you fix fast, bargain constantly, and keep the family (democracy) living together. That is exactly the spirit behind the next five sections.
]

= The Legacy of Partition: Resettling the Refugees

Partition was the loudest birth-cry of the two countries. The boundary award split Bengal in the east and Punjab in the west through mixed, living communities — so millions woke up on the "wrong" side of a line drawn by a barrister who had never visited India.

- *The human scale:*
  - *Communal violence* swept both sides of the border; lakhs of people were killed and huge areas of Punjab, Bengal, Delhi and the United Provinces saw burning villages and trains of the dead.
  - *About 80 lakh refugees* crossed INTO India from Pakistan — the largest single migration of the twentieth century; a matching flow went the other way.
  - *Who they were:* Punjabi farmers and shopkeepers from west Pakistan; Sindhi traders; Bengali families from the east — entire occupations and neighbourhoods uprooted at once.

- *The resettlement challenge — bigger than shelter:*
  - *Physical survival first:* refugee camps in Delhi, Calcutta, Jalandhar and Kurukshetra ran on famine rations through 1947-48; cholera and winter killed the weakest.
  - *Jobs and land:* the west-flowing refugees were farmers arriving on OTHER people's land — India responded with land allotments, compensation bonds and new townships (Faridabad grew out of a camp; Sindhi refugees built whole new markets).
  - *Identity and citizenship:* refugees had lost homes, documents and sometimes graves of ancestors — the state's answer was fast citizenship and the promise that India, not Pakistan, was now their nation.
  - *The political meaning:* the resettlement effort happened while the Constitution was being drafted — it hardened the secular commitment: India would be the homeland of ALL its citizens, of every faith, and Muslims in India were NOT second-class members.

- *Gandhi's answer to the riots:*
  - While Calcutta burned in September 1947, the 77-year-old Gandhi *walked its streets unarmed and fasted* until Hindu, Muslim and Sikh leaders signed a peace pledge — violence stopped in days.
  - *30 January 1948:* a Hindu nationalist assassinated him — the price of the peace he preached. The martyrdom strengthened the young republic's resolve for a secular state.

= Integrating 565 Princely States

The British Crown had ruled about 45% of India directly; the rest sat under 565 princes who owed allegiance to the Crown — and with the Crown gone, each one could technically walk away. A subcontinent of independent kingdoms would have meant a thousand borders, customs posts and wars. Fusing them into the Union by 15 August 1947 is the least-told miracle of 1947.

- *The tool — the Instrument of Accession:*
  - Rulers ceded just THREE subjects to the Union: *defence, external affairs and communications* — everything else stayed theirs for the moment. Limited surrender made signing easy.
  - *The team:* Sardar Patel as Deputy Prime Minister + *V.P. Menon* as the tireless civil-service negotiator — running the States Department, flying state to state, offering carrots (privy purses, titles) and gentle sticks (no participation in the Union's institutions).
  - *The result:* by 15 August 1947 almost all states had signed. Travel from Delhi to Madras no longer needed a passport.

- *Three holdouts became exam legends:*
  - *Junagadh — the plebiscite route:* a Muslim ruler (the Nawab) governed a Hindu-majority state next to Pakistan's coast. He acceded to PAKISTAN; India blockaded, the Nawab fled, and in February *1948 a plebiscite* voted overwhelmingly for India. Majority sentiment won.
  - *Hyderabad — the police action:* the Nizam — India's richest man — wanted independence, while his paramilitary *Razakars* terrorised villagers. After 13 months of failed talks, India launched *"police action"* in September 1948; the Nizam signed within days. Force, used once, precisely.
  - *Kashmir — the unfinished story:* the only Muslim-majority state with a Hindu ruler — and a border touching both new countries. Detailed in the next section.

- *The north-eastern edge:*
  - *Manipur and Tripura:* princely states merged through negotiated agreements in 1949 after popular movements pressed for representative government.
  - *The Naga hills:* Naga leaders had already declared independence (1947); the area became a CENTRALLY administered territory and only became the state of *Nagaland in 1963* — the first of the north-eastern states. Integration here took patience that the west never needed.

#formula-box(title: "The Three Holdouts on One Line Each")[
  - *Junagadh:* Hindu majority + Muslim ruler → plebiscite (Feb 1948) → India.

  - *Hyderabad:* landlocked giant + Nizam's Razakars → police action (Sept 1948) → accession.

  - *Kashmir:* Muslim majority + Hindu ruler + tribal invasion (Oct 1947) → Hari Singh accedes → war and permanent dispute.
]

= The Kashmir Problem: The Dispute That Never Closed

Kashmir mattered beyond its size because BOTH new countries claimed it — India by the ruler's legal accession, Pakistan by its two-nation logic (a Muslim-majority area should be Pakistan's). The collision produced the subcontinent's longest-running dispute.

- *The accession sequence (learn it as a chain):*
  - *The setting, 1947:* Maharaja *Hari Singh* — a Hindu ruler of a Muslim-majority state — hesitated between India, Pakistan and independence; *Sheikh Abdullah's* National Conference, the state's popular movement, leaned toward India.
  - *October 1947:* Pashtun *tribal invaders*, backed by Pakistan, poured toward Srinagar, looting as they came. Hari Singh, his option of neutrality destroyed, signed the *Instrument of Accession* (26 October 1947).
  - *The airlift:* Indian troops flew into Srinagar and pushed the raiders back from the valley.
  - *1948 — internationalisation:* India took the question to the UN (January 1948); a *ceasefire (January 1949)* froze the battle lines into a *ceasefire line* — today's Line of Control — leaving roughly a third of the territory Pakistani-administered.

- *Inside the Indian framework:*
  - *Article 370:* because the accession was limited (defence, external affairs, communications), the Constitution gave Jammu & Kashmir *special status* — its own constitution and autonomy over internal matters.
  - *Delhi Agreement (1952):* Nehru and Sheikh Abdullah settled the state's relationship with the Union — Abdullah wanted maximal autonomy; disagreement followed, and in *1953 he was dismissed and jailed* — the first rupture between Srinagar and Delhi.
  - *The wars:* 1947-48, 1965, 1971 (and later conflict beyond this chapter) — each froze the same line but never settled the claim.
  - *The two dimensions (the exam-ready phrase):* an *external* dimension (India-Pakistan rivalry over the territory) and an *internal* dimension (how much autonomy the state should enjoy) — both trace back to 1947.

#analogy-box(title: "The House With Two Deeds")[
  Think of a house sold under pressure during a burglary. India's deed = the Instrument of Accession the owner (Hari Singh) actually signed — legally airtight. Pakistan's claim = the neighbourhood's majority logic — "the area should belong to us by population." The burglary (tribal invasion) forced the sale, the police (Indian army) arrived after signing, and the property line (ceasefire line) froze where the fight stopped. Both sides still wave their papers seventy years on — which is why the dispute outlived every generation of negotiators.
]

= Organisation and Reorganisation of States

Independent India inherited a chaotic internal map: British provinces drawn for colonial convenience, princely states of every size, and four categories of "accustomed" entities inside the Union. Redrawing the map on DEMOCRATIC lines — the people's languages, not the Raj's revenue maps — was the next nation-building battle, and it went through refusals, a martyrdom and a commission.

- *Why language mattered so much:*
  - The Congress itself had promised *linguistic provinces since 1921* — its provincial committees were organised on language lines. The demand was democratic, not disruptive.
  - But *Partition changed the mood:* a country torn apart on religious lines now feared that redrawing states on language lines would start the same infection. Unity came first — initially.

- *The two refusals (1948-49):*
  - *Dhar Commission (1948):* examined the question right after independence and ruled that *administrative convenience*, NOT language, should decide state boundaries.
  - *JVP Committee (1948-49):* the Congress's own panel — *Jawaharlal Nehru, Vallabhbhai Patel, Pattabhi Sitaramayya* — reached the same verdict: language as the basis was too dangerous for the moment.
  - *The effect:* public feeling in Telugu, Tamil, Kannada and Marathi regions kept building regardless — refusals could slow the demand but not kill it.

- *The martyrdom — Potti Sriramulu:*
  - A Gandhi-era freedom fighter, Sriramulu *fasted for a Telugu state* separated from Madras. He died on *15 December 1952* — day 58 of the fast.
  - *The explosion:* riots and public fury across the Telugu country forced Delhi's hand within days.
  - *Andhra State (October 1953):* carved out of Madras — the FIRST linguistic state of India, with Kurnool as capital. The dam had cracked.
  - *The lesson Delhi learned:* ignoring democratic linguistic feeling was more dangerous than accepting it.

- *The States Reorganisation Act (1956) — the big map:*
  - *The commission:* the *States Reorganisation Commission (SRC)* — *Fazl Ali, K.M. Panikkar, H.N. Kunzru* — set up in 1953, reported in 1955. It accepted language as a basis BUT with unity safeguards: national integrity first, administrative viability second.
  - *The Act (August 1956, effective 1 November 1956):* *14 states and 6 union territories* — the map finally spoke the people's languages (Madras, Mysore, Bombay, Punjab... each roughly a language zone).
  - *After-shocks (each one a favourite question):*
    - *Bombay split (1960):* Marathi and Gujarati agitation broke the bilingual giant into *Maharashtra and Gujarat*.
    - *Nagaland (1963):* the special north-eastern accommodation.
    - *Punjab reorganisation (1966):* the Punjabi Suba movement (Akali Dal) won a Punjabi-speaking state; *Haryana* was carved out and hill areas went to Himachal Pradesh.

