= Detailed Solutions

Each solution shows the reasoning, not just the verdict — cover the answer line, attempt the question, then compare.

#solution(1, [The States Department under Patel and Menon negotiated with about 565 princely states — the number the NCERT and exam sources fix. 552 signed by August 1947; the rest followed through 1948.], answer: [B])

#solution(2, [Sardar Patel (Deputy PM) and V.P. Menon (States Department) ran the integration — persuasion, privy purses, and the Instrument of Accession. Nehru handled the wider vision; the Sardar handled the map.], answer: [D])

#solution(3, [Andhra State — carved from Madras after Potti Sriramulu's 58-day fast ended in death on 15 December 1952. Kerala, Maharashtra and Punjab all became (or were reorganised as) states only in 1956 or later.], answer: [C])

#solution(4, [The Act gave effect to the SRC's map: 14 states and 6 union territories from 1 November 1956. The 16-state and 25-state figures belong to later decades of reorganisation.], answer: [A])

#solution(5, [The Dhar Commission put administrative convenience first and refused language as the basis — the JVP Committee (Nehru-Patel-Pattabhi) confirmed that refusal in 1948-49.], answer: [B])

#solution(6, [Maharaja Hari Singh signed on 26 October 1947 as tribal raiders backed by Pakistan advanced on Srinagar; Indian troops were airlifted the next morning. Gulab Singh was the dynasty's 19th-century founder — a dynasty distractor.], answer: [A])

#solution(7, [Article 370 embodied the special status that flowed from Kashmir's LIMITED accession (defence, external affairs, communications). Articles 356 and 365 are President's rule provisions — constitutional distractors.], answer: [C])

#solution(8, [The January 1965 agitation swept Madras state when the 1965 deadline for English's phase-out approached; self-immolations and deaths forced the Centre to keep English. Kerala, Andhra and Mysore were not the epicentre.], answer: [D])

#solution(9, [Marathi and Gujarati agitations split bilingual Bombay into Maharashtra and Gujarat on 1 May 1960. Karnataka and Rajasthan were never part of that state.], answer: [B])

